# Chapter 8 — Fine-Tuning and Assembled Laws

*A condition can be older than the thing it explains and still have a history of its own.*

The physical argument has moved from discovery and steam engines to questions about existence, differentiation, and gradients. We now ask whether the rules and parameter values that those processes inherit could also have developmental explanations. The working conjecture is that the universe and its fundamental constants are probably assembled. Here probably expresses a provisional judgment, not a calculated probability.

The emergence of the universe is proposed as a natural process. The question is how physical organization could produce some of the conditions that later organization requires. A successful account must identify the relevant relations and transitions while leaving room for whatever contingency its dynamics permits.

The two parts of the conjecture require different achievements. Assembling the universe concerns the emergence of its physical organization, potentially including spacetime and the distinctions through which subsequent events become possible. Assembling fundamental constants concerns explaining the physical relations and values represented by those constants. A constant would be an outcome of the account rather than an unexplained setting supplied to it. A history of effective properties alone would leave this stronger claim unresolved.

A useful entry into these questions is a much smaller physical event, one whose conditions we can change ourselves.

Put a coin in a bowl and step back until the rim hides it. Keep your head still while someone adds water. From a suitable position, the coin comes back into view. The coin has not moved. The water has changed the route by which light from it can reach your eye.

The demonstration is simple enough to perform in a kitchen. Its explanation opens a much larger problem. A change in the material between two places changes the path connecting them. What appears to be a property of the route belongs partly to the environment through which the route passes.

In the language of geometrical optics, light bends when it passes obliquely between materials in which it travels at different speeds. Fermat's principle describes the relevant path by comparing travel times along nearby alternatives. For a simple crossing between air and water, the physical route minimizes the time between fixed endpoints. More generally, the precise requirement is stationarity: a sufficiently small change of path produces no first-order change in travel time.[1]

That last distinction deserves a little patience. At the bottom of a smooth valley, a small step in either direction changes height less rapidly than a step on the slope. The immediate rate of change is zero. A hilltop also has that property. Stationarity concerns this local condition, which need not identify the lowest value available anywhere. The familiar expression “least time” is memorable, but its apparent simplicity can conceal what the principle says.

For the bowl, compare two imagined routes between the coin and the same point above the water. One might use a shorter stretch in air but a longer stretch in water. Another does the reverse. Each travel time depends on both distances and speeds. The shortest distance and the shortest time need not describe the same route.

We can calculate such routes by varying the crossing point at the surface. Nothing about that calculation requires the light to make the comparison consciously. The alternatives belong to our description of the problem. The material world supplies the interactions whose behavior the description captures.

This matters because the language of optimization invites a seductive mistake. We encounter an elegant principle and imagine that nature has a goal. We see a constrained path and give the universe the intentions of an engineer. But a mathematical extremum is not an intention hidden inside matter.

The lesson from the bowl is already considerable without that addition. To understand the path, we must understand the conditions through which it passes. Change the conditions and the path changes, although the general principle used to describe it remains applicable.

The book began by asking what constraints make possible and what maintains them. Here is a small answer. Some constraints belong to the arrangement of other things. The surface of the water is one such arrangement. It was absent before the water entered the bowl. It can be moved, disturbed, or removed. The law used to explain the bending did not have to be invented when the bowl was filled, but this particular condition of the light's passage did.

A constraint can be real without being eternal.

There are therefore at least two questions hidden inside the question about origins. How does an environment acquire a condition that affects later events? And why are there laws under which environments and events behave as they do? An answer to the first may leave the second untouched. It can still explain something important.

Physics repeatedly makes progress by finding that what was treated as a condition at one level can be described as a process at another. The danger is to stop too soon and call every condition fundamental. The opposite danger is to announce that all conditions have been explained because one of them has a history.

The title of this chapter names the possibility between those dangers. Perhaps some features that look as though they had to be specified in advance were instead reached through development. To investigate that possibility, we need to know what the established physics permits us to say, and where the proposed extension begins.

The principle of stationary action provides a useful discipline. In a familiar class of mechanical problems, action is the time integral of kinetic energy minus potential energy. The physical trajectory makes the first-order change in this quantity vanish under small variations that preserve the specified endpoints. For those systems, this condition gives the same equations of motion as the familiar force-based account.[2]

Action is not an amount of usable energy waiting to be extracted. It has units of energy multiplied by time. Its stationarity says nothing, by itself, about the fastest route to a more productive civilization. Nor is the physical trajectory necessarily the global minimum of action. Importing “least” into an argument about history without importing the defined quantity, the allowed variations, and the boundary conditions leaves only the sound of a physical principle.

Yet the achievement of the principle should not be diminished. The same motion can be understood through local changes and through a condition applied to a whole path. A description that sounds purposeful can be mathematically connected to one that does not invoke purpose at all.

This suggests a standard for the larger argument. If we say that development tends toward greater access to energy, we owe an account of the interactions that produce that tendency. Which organizations persist? What changes their reproductive prospects? Which pathways become accessible? A comparison with stationary action may inspire those questions. It cannot answer them on their behalf.

There is a difference between discovering order and assigning it a destination.

Before we ask whether physical constants could have histories, we need one more distinction: a law can be reliable without being the deepest available description.

The stiffness of a material is a measurable property. It constrains how the material responds to force. But it belongs to an organized collection of constituents, not to a single isolated atom carrying a tiny copy of the material's rigidity. Change the arrangement or conditions and the collective response can change. A calculation at the scale of the whole material can be useful without tracking every microscopic motion.

Effective theories formalize the possibility of working at a specified scale while retaining the influence of smaller-scale processes in suitable parameters and interactions. Their success is not a declaration that the ignored details do not exist. It depends on understanding which effects remain important for the question being asked. Polchinski's account of effective field theory makes this discipline explicit.[3]

This gives a precise alternative to treating every stable regularity as an isolated commandment. Some regularities belong to a regime. They can be explained through the structure and scale at which they arise. Their dependence on a deeper account does not make them illusory.

But changing the scale of a description is not the same event as changing a law over historical time. The scale dependence of a parameter in a physical calculation does not by itself establish that its measured value has drifted through cosmic history. Likewise, a new collective property of matter does not prove that the most fundamental interactions were assembled from earlier ones. These are different extensions, with different evidence required.

The distinction lets us state the ambition without inflating it. We already have reasons to ask whether a regularity is fundamental or effective. The further proposal is that some conditions now treated as fixed may be outcomes of physical development. To evaluate that proposal, we must name the condition and specify the development.

This brings us to the fine-tuning debate.

In a common form of the argument, a model contains parameters whose values affect whether familiar complex structures can exist. Vary a parameter, or several, and examine the consequences. Perhaps a reaction no longer occurs in the same way, a bound structure becomes unstable, or the history of cosmic expansion changes so that certain structures cannot form. The apparent sensitivity motivates the question: why are the values compatible with the world we observe?

That is a legitimate question. It is not yet a calculation of the probability that such a world would exist.

A probability requires more than a range of numbers on a page. We need a distribution, a reason for choosing it, and an account of which quantities can vary independently. Assigning equal weight to equal intervals of a quantity does not generally assign equal weight to equal intervals of its logarithm. A sensitivity study can be informative even when there is no agreed physical mechanism supplying the distribution of possible values. The two tasks should not be confused.

An elementary example shows why independence matters. Imagine two quantities that must be nearly equal for a device to work. If each is independently set somewhere in a broad range, close agreement occupies only a narrow region of the possibilities. But suppose a linkage forces the second quantity to follow the first. Agreement can then occur throughout a large range of settings. What looked like two fortunate choices becomes one relation requiring an explanation.

The example does not demonstrate that physical constants are linked in the needed way. It shows what a developmental proposal would have to discover. A correlation cannot simply be announced because it would solve the problem. The linkage must exist, and its operation must account for the required relation without hiding the same unexplained precision elsewhere.

The cosmological constant makes the demand especially severe. In general relativity, a constant term in the gravitational equations can be represented as a contribution with the character of uniform vacuum energy. A positive value can produce accelerated cosmic expansion. The supernova studies published by the Riess and Perlmutter teams in the late 1990s supplied major evidence for acceleration.[4]

The observation of acceleration and the explanation of its cause remain different questions. A cosmological constant is a possible description of the accelerating component; discussing its theoretical problem does not establish that all dark-energy behavior has been settled. The issue relevant here is why a vacuum contribution compatible with cosmology is so small compared with scales suggested by particle physics.

Quantum fields and other contributions enter the vacuum-energy accounting. The challenge is not simply that one rough calculation prints a spectacularly large number. It is how a small net gravitational effect can remain small when the theory includes much larger contributions and corrections. The often-repeated numerical disparity depends on the calculation and its assumptions; it should not be treated as a unique, context-free prediction of quantum mechanics.[5]

A proposed developmental explanation must face that accounting. If a field changes so as to reduce one contribution, what determines its stopping point? If contributions cancel, what maintains the cancellation when other physics changes? If a mechanism drives the result toward zero, does it also explain a small value compatible with the observed expansion? Calling the outcome stable answers none of those questions until the stability is derived.

Observer selection offers a different kind of explanation. We cannot observe our environment from a place in which no observers can arise. This conditions the observations available to us. In a 1987 argument, Steven Weinberg investigated an anthropic bound associated with the formation of gravitationally bound structures: an excessively large positive cosmological constant could prevent such structures from forming under the conditions considered.[6]

The importance of that argument lies partly in its specificity. It connected an observational prerequisite with physical development, rather than merely saying that the universe must suit us because we are here. But conditioning on an observer's existence is not the same operation as constructing the environment in which the observer exists.

If many environments are proposed, their distribution still needs an account. If only one environment is considered, the fact that we could not inhabit an incompatible one does not identify the mechanism that gave this one its properties. Observer selection, dynamical explanation, and the description of an initial condition answer related but different questions. None should win by silently adopting the question best suited to it.

The developmental alternative pursued in this book treats fundamental constants as probably assembled outcomes. Its proposed sequence is a drive acting through constraints, a resulting organization changing those constraints, and further development occurring within the changed possibilities. The eventual result might then be less sensitive to some initial differences than a picture of independently selected settings suggests. Extending the account to the universe itself requires explaining the organization in which this sequence becomes meaningful.

Five strands of the argument can now be joined into a material account of fine-tuning. Their proposed connection is that the conditions for an enduring world could be produced through the world's development. The physical and the Platonic aspects would emerge together: material configurations realize relations that constrain subsequent configurations, and those relations help determine which further material organizations can form.

First, constraints can progressively narrow the accessible configurations along a developmental history. Once a boundary, interaction, or stable relation exists, some continuations become unavailable and others become much easier to reach. A mathematical inventory of independently adjustable settings may then count combinations the physical process could never produce. Narrowing concerns the continuations available under those conditions; an assembly can also open new possibilities that were previously inaccessible. The claim is about the organization of accessible paths, not a universal shrinking of every physical state space.

Second, least-time and, more generally, stationary-action principles show how physical trajectories can obey precise relations without an agent comparing every imaginable route. In their applicable settings, these principles express the dynamics together with specified conditions. They give the proposed synthesis a model of physically realized constraint, while leaving the origin of the action and its parameters to be explained. Stationarity itself does not select a unique universe or establish a maximum rate of entropy production.[2]

Third, symmetry breaking can produce stable physical configurations with definite properties. A symmetric description can admit several outcomes, while interactions and conditions lead a system into a particular organized state. That state supplies conditions for later events. This offers a concrete way for an initially available range of behavior to acquire a persistent structure. To extend the mechanism to fundamental parameter values, the account must show which properties are determined, which remain contingent, and what selects among the possible states.[7]

Fourth, recursively self-maintaining organization can preserve conditions that a transient configuration would lose. Autopoiesis supplies the demanding example in which operations regenerate the organization that makes those operations possible. The proposed cosmological extension would identify corresponding dependencies: which processes maintain which structures, and how those structures sustain further processes. Stable persistence is only the beginning of that explanation. The recursive relationships and their physical support must be specified before they can explain the continuation of a cosmic organization.

Fifth, accumulated structures change the conditions for subsequent structures. Development can retain an achievement and use it in a later transformation. The next event therefore occurs within an organization partly produced by earlier events. In the fine-tuning hypothesis, some relations that appear remarkably coordinated when viewed as independent settings would instead be connected outcomes of this cumulative history.

Taken together, these mechanisms suggest a plausible path by which fine-tuning could have been a material and practical process: transformations occurring through available interactions, stabilizing some outcomes, maintaining some of their conditions, and making further transformations accessible. Here practical means physically achievable and capable of continuing under its actual conditions. The proposed explanation must eventually account for rates, barriers, stability, and the resources required for each transition.

The claim is that the emergence of the universe could be a natural process in which apparently coordinated conditions develop together. Such a process can contain contingencies, alternative outcomes, and failed transitions. A model might show convergence from a broad range of starting conditions or explain a particular dependency without selecting a unique cosmic history. The task is to derive what the process produces, including which features remain sensitive to its starting conditions.

This challenges the inference that peculiar constants make the universe intrinsically unlikely. Peculiarity alone supplies neither a probability distribution nor evidence that the constants were independently chosen. A successful developmental model could overturn an improbability estimate based on those assumptions by showing how the relevant relations are physically generated. The mechanisms assembled here motivate that alternative; they have not already derived the observed constants or resolved every physical fine-tuning problem.[8]

That is a stronger proposal than a resemblance between a universe and a factory. It would require a dynamical model in which a parameter or regularity changes, the relevant entropy production can be defined, and the observed outcome follows from a range of starting states. If the model merely inserts a preference for our observed constant, it has encoded the answer rather than explained it. If it derives a tendency toward another value, the theory has made a substantive prediction and faces a substantive failure.

The questions resemble those raised by convergent discovery, although the mechanisms need not match. Which alternatives are actually accessible? How much dependence on initial conditions remains? Do different histories approach a comparable outcome? In physics these questions require dynamics and observables. The recurrence of a scientific idea cannot serve as evidence for a distribution of cosmic constants.

There are concrete proposals in theoretical physics that give this kind of question mathematical form. In 2015, Peter Graham, David Kaplan, and Surjeet Rajendran proposed a mechanism in which early-universe dynamics could help establish the electroweak scale. An evolving field changes the effective Higgs mass parameter; the development of a Higgs vacuum value affects barriers that can stop the field's motion. Their proposal is often called the relaxion mechanism.[9]

This is a proposed solution to a particular hierarchy problem, not an experimentally established history of the universe. It introduces additional physics and demanding cosmological conditions. It does not solve the cosmological constant problem merely because it concerns a scale set through cosmic evolution. Its relevance here is methodological: it specifies changing quantities, interactions, and a stopping process. A developmental claim has become something whose assumptions and consequences can be examined.

That is the standard the larger proposal must meet.

Suppose we describe a quantity as settling into a stable range. We should be able to say what changes it, what resists the change, and how a perturbation affects the result. We should ask whether many starting conditions reach the range or only a specially prepared few. We should identify observations that would differ if the mechanism were absent. An attractive story of self-adjustment becomes a physical explanation only by taking on these obligations.

There is a particular temptation to import the organization of living things into this account. An organism maintains conditions for its continuation by producing and repairing parts of the system that makes continuation possible. Autopoiesis names the demanding kind of self-production examined in chapter 3. It can suggest a question about recursive stabilization at other scales.

But a field settling into a stable state is not thereby an organism producing its own boundary. Nor does ordinary persistence establish autopoiesis. If we say that the universe maintains itself, we must identify what is produced, which organization is maintained, and what distinguishes the claim from the fact that physical processes continue. Otherwise the biological comparison supplies a feeling of explanation while assuming the organization whose origin is in question.

The strongest objection is circularity. Any developmental model already contains some rules under which development occurs. If those rules require just the parameters we hoped to explain, the problem has merely changed address. If the mechanism needs a specially prepared starting point, its explanatory gain depends on whether that preparation is less demanding or independently justified.

Yet the existence of assumptions does not make every developmental explanation empty. Explaining one quantity through relations among others can be real progress. Deriving an outcome across a wider range of initial conditions can be real progress. Finding a new observable consequence can be real progress. The standard is whether an explanation reduces an identifiable problem and survives investigation, not whether it begins by explaining why anything at all exists.

Materialism need not promise an impossible view from outside the world. Its commitment here is to seek relations within the world that account for what the world can do. This includes taking organization seriously, rather than treating every collective capacity as a mystery added to otherwise complete isolated parts.

It also means applying the same standards to our own language. “The universe assembled it” must not become the physical equivalent of “someone arranged it” with the someone left unnamed. Assembly needs components or processes, accessible transformations, and an account of what persists. At the deepest level, even those categories may require revision. Their usefulness in chemistry or history cannot guarantee their ultimate status.

We can now state the chapter's established starting point and its proposed extension separately. Physical development does produce conditions that become the constraints of later events. Collective states alter behavior; effective descriptions capture regularities at particular scales; pathways and barriers shape what can occur. These are substantial reasons to question whether every enabling condition should be pictured as an independently chosen setting at the beginning.

Those results do not establish that fundamental constants or the universe were assembled. The book pursues assembly as a proposed natural account of their emergence, through progressive constraint, stabilization, recursive maintenance, and cumulative development. Stationary principles help articulate physical relations within such an account. The manuscript has not derived the values of constants, the initial low-entropy condition, or existence itself. Explaining their formation would be a substantive achievement even if other histories remained possible.

The extension also reaches the meaning of assembly. If spacetime belongs to what is assembled, an account cannot quietly borrow an external clock to arrange its stages. It must specify the relations or ordering through which development is defined. If it begins with an energy difference, it must say what physical organization makes that difference possible. Naming a precursor does not finish the explanation; it identifies the next condition whose status the theory must confront.

A material history can be ambitious without claiming that every unanswered question has the same answer.

Return to the bowl. The light's path depends on the water, and the water's presence has a history. We can explain that dependence without explaining why physical law exists. We can then investigate the material that refracts the light, the organization of its constituents, and the conditions that made that organization possible. Each successful step replaces part of a presumed background with an account of how things relate.

The return to the bowl gives the entire argument a shape. Material organization changes which formal route is realized. An earlier physical history made that organization available. A mind can investigate the relation, preserve it in a theory, and use it to build another instrument. An artificial system may eventually participate in extending that inquiry. Each achievement becomes a condition of another.

The book proposes that disequilibrium-driven maximization of energy extraction and entropy production supplies a common physical cause beneath this sequence. Assembly names the production of its pathways. Dialectical transformation names the reorganization by which inherited capabilities and constraints are preserved, negated, and changed. Convergent discovery is proposed as a sign that some routes have become strongly favored. These are connected claims, not interchangeable definitions.

The available historical constraint concerns a much narrower issue. Oklo's ancient natural reactors constrain certain proposed later changes in the fine-structure constant under stated nuclear-model assumptions. The research script records a published bound; it does not fit raw isotope data or infer the origin of constants. Stability after formation can constrain a model that predicts subsequent drift while leaving its formation mechanism unresolved.[10]

The causal standard applies here as well. Historical observations can test a physical origin model when competing accounts imply distinguishable observations under stated assumptions. We cannot treat different galaxies as universes assigned different fundamental constants, or infer the origin of a constant from an experiment on particles governed by that constant. Where the proposed origins remain observationally indistinguishable, the causal claim remains unresolved. The afterword specifies this limit without requiring that cosmological history be rerun.

The creation of existence remains the deepest outstanding problem. An account that begins with gradients still owes an account of their availability; an account that begins with formal possibilities still owes an account of their status. The cosmological constant, the low-entropy past, and the origin of fundamental laws are not solved here. They specify what a universal version of the hypothesis must eventually explain.

Yet the inquiry has gained something by following the same question all the way back. It can no longer treat the scientific mind as outside material history, intelligence as an inexplicable visitor to matter, or the conditions of a productive system as eternally supplied. It must explain how its own instruments of explanation came to exist.

The physical section ends with this question still open. It has moved from the discovery of formal relations through the construction of pathways to the conjectured assembly of physical conditions, while identifying a different evidentiary burden at each step. The next chapter begins the human history of the instruments of explanation themselves: how cooperation, care, restraint, and learning could change the conditions under which people develop. The argument will then follow collective knowledge, economic self-production, self-modeling, and AI within the physical world whose origins remain to be explained.

