1. Richard Feynman, Robert Leighton, and Matthew Sands, [The Feynman Lectures on Physics, I.26](https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_26.html), especially sections 26–3 and 26–5. The bowl is an illustrative demonstration, not a reported event. The discussion uses the geometrical-optics approximation and a simple stationary interface.

2. Feynman, Leighton, and Sands, [II.19, “The Principle of Least Action”](https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_19.html). The kinetic-minus-potential expression applies to the familiar mechanical setting described, not to every possible physical system. Stationarity is the general point at issue.

3. Joseph Polchinski, [“Effective Field Theory and the Fermi Surface”](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9210046), TASI 1992 lectures, revised 1999. The discussion distinguishes scale-dependent description from change over cosmic time.

4. Adam G. Riess et al., [“Observational Evidence from Supernovae for an Accelerating Universe and a Cosmological Constant”](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9805201) (1998); Saul Perlmutter et al., [“Measurements of Omega and Lambda from 42 High-Redshift Supernovae”](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9812133) (1999).

5. Sean M. Carroll, [“The Cosmological Constant”](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0004075) (2001); Jérôme Martin, [“Everything You Always Wanted To Know About The Cosmological Constant Problem (But Were Afraid To Ask)”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.3365) (2012). Used for the theoretical problem rather than present-day parameter estimates.

6. Steven Weinberg, [“Anthropic Bound on the Cosmological Constant”](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.2607) (1987). The chapter distinguishes this conditional argument from a demonstrated distribution of universes or a dynamical origin of the constant.

7. David Tong, [Statistical Field Theory, section 1, “From Spins to Fields”](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/sft/sfthtml/S1.html). The magnetic example is an idealization; strict spontaneous symmetry breaking involves the thermodynamic limit, while actual finite samples have additional conditions.

8. **Material fine-tuning synthesis.** The combination of progressive constraints, stationary principles, symmetry breaking, recursive maintenance, and cumulative assembly is the book's proposal, preserved in I66. Its component sources include [S05](sources.md#s05), [S22](sources.md#s22), [S29](sources.md#s29), and [S33](sources.md#s33). The relaxion proposal concerns a specified hierarchy problem; neither it nor the other sources establishes the combined account of cosmic emergence. The afterword requires explicit dynamics and distinguishing observable consequences.

9. Peter W. Graham, David E. Kaplan, and Surjeet Rajendran, [“Cosmological Relaxation of the Electroweak Scale”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.07551) (2015). The relaxion is presented as a proposed mechanism with additional assumptions, not an established cosmic history. The broader developmental synthesis and the linkage illustration are arguments of this book.

